939 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 627 0 R
/E (The liver is a vital organ responsible for maintaining metabolic homeostasis, detoxifying harmful substances, and regulating essential biochemical processes in the body \(Verma et al., 2022\). Due to its central role in metabolism and detoxification, the liver is highly susceptible to damage caused by toxins, drugs, alcohol and viral infections \(Chung et al., 2022\). Persistent injury to liver cells can lead to chronic conditions such as liver cirrhosis, a severe and irreversible disease characterized by fibrosis, structural distortion, and progressive loss of hepatic function \(Friedman, 2021; Bataller et al., 2022\). Cirrhosis not only compromises normal liver activity but also increases the risk of life-threatening complications, including portal hypertension and liver failure \(Chung et al., 2022\). Despite advances in medical science, current treatments for liver cirrhosis remain largely supportive rather than curative \(Rao et al., 2020\). Many synthetic drugs used in liver therapy are associated with adverse effects and limited long-term efficacy, highlighting the need for safer and more effective alternatives \(Li et al., 2022\). In recent years, there has been a growing interest in medicinal plants as potential sources of hepatoprotective agents due to their bioactive compounds and minimal side effects \(Verma et al., 2022; Bansal et al., 2022\). Pergularia daemia, a traditionally valued medicinal plant, has been widely used in herbal medicine for treating inflammatory and hepatic disorders \(Devi et al., 2021\). It is rich in phytoconstituents such as flavonoids, alkaloids, and saponins, which are known for their antioxidant and cytoprotective properties \(Devi et al., 2021\). These compounds may help in neutralizing free radicals and protecting liver cells from damage \(Gupta et al., 2021\). Diosgenin, a naturally occurring steroidal saponin, is well recognized for its hepatoprotective and antioxidant activities and is often used as a standard compound in experimental studies \(Patel et al., 2022; Kumar et al., 2021\). In this context, the present study aims to investigate the hepatocyte protective activity of Pergularia daemia using an in-vitro model of liver toxicity and to compare its effectiveness with Diosgenin, thereby exploring its potential as a natural therapeutic agent for liver cirrhosis. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
