/E (The liver is a highly specialized and multifunctional organ that plays a central role in maintaining physiological balance within the human body. It is responsible for a wide array of functions, including metabolism of carbohydrates, proteins, and lipids, detoxification of harmful substances, storage of essential nutrients, and synthesis of vital proteins such as albumin and clotting factors \(Verma et al., 2022\). Additionally, the liver produces bile, which is essential for digestion and absorption of dietary fats. Due to its strategic position and functional complexity, the liver is continuously exposed to various endogenous and exogenous toxins, making it particularly susceptible to injury \(Koyama et al., 2021; Tilg et al., 2022\). Chronic liver damage can arise from multiple factors such as excessive alcohol consumption, viral infections like hepatitis B and C, prolonged use of hepatotoxic drugs, and metabolic disorders \(Bataller et al., 2022\). Over time, repeated injury leads to inflammation and activation of hepatic stellate cells, resulting in the accumulation of extracellular matrix proteins and fibrosis \(Chung et al., 2022\). As fibrosis progresses, it disrupts the normal architecture of the liver, eventually leading to cirrhosis. Liver cirrhosis is characterized by the formation of regenerative nodules surrounded by fibrotic tissue, which impairs blood flow and reduces functional capacity. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
946 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 628 0 R
/T (Figure 1:)
/E (Figure 1:)
/Pg 23 0 R
