949 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 629 0 R
/E (The clinical consequences of cirrhosis are severe and include complications such as portal hypertension, ascites, hepatic encephalopathy, and an increased risk of hepatocellular carcinoma \(Chung et al., 2022\). Since cirrhosis is often diagnosed at an advanced stage, its management becomes challenging. Therefore, understanding the underlying mechanisms of liver damage and identifying effective protective strategies are essential steps toward reducing the burden of liver diseases and improving patient outcomes \(Singh et al., 2023; Parola et al., 2022\). )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
950 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 629 0 R
/E (The treatment of liver cirrhosis remains a significant challenge in modern medicine due to the complex and progressive nature of the disease. Current therapeutic strategies are primarily aimed at controlling symptoms, slowing disease progression, and preventing complications rather than achieving complete recovery. Pharmacological interventions include antiviral agents for viral hepatitis, corticosteroids for inflammatory conditions, and diuretics to manage fluid accumulation \(Li et al., 2022\). While these treatments can provide temporary relief and improve quality of life, they often fail to reverse the underlying structural damage to the liver. One of the major drawbacks of conventional therapies is the potential for adverse side effects, particularly with long-term use. Many synthetic drugs used in liver disease management can exert additional stress on the liver, sometimes exacerbating the condition they are intended to treat \(Li et al., 2022\). Drug interactions and toxicity further complicate treatment, especially in patients with advanced liver dysfunction. Moreover, the high cost of long-term medication and regular monitoring places a financial burden on patients, limiting accessibility for many individuals. In cases of end-stage liver disease, liver transplantation is considered the only definitive treatment \(Younossi et al., 2022\). However, this option is constrained by several factors, including the scarcity of donor organs, high surgical risks, and the requirement for lifelong immunosuppressive therapy to prevent organ rejection. These limitations highlight the urgent need for alternative therapeutic approaches that are not only effective but also safe, affordable, and accessible. Consequently, there isgrowing interest in exploring natural products and herbal medicines as potential hepatoprotective agents that can complement or substitute conventional treatments. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
