1007 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 633 0 R
/E (The scope of the present study lies in exploring the hepatoprotective potential of Pergularia daemia through controlled in-vitro experimental approaches. This research focuses on assessing the ability of the plant extract to protect hepatocyte cells from chemically induced toxicity, thereby providing preliminary scientific evidence for its use in liver-related disorders. By employing established cell line models and biochemical assays, the study offers a reliable platform to evaluate cellular responses, enzyme activity, and oxidative stress under simulated cirrhotic conditions. The findings of this work can contribute to a deeper understanding of the therapeutic value of medicinal plants in hepatology. It opens avenues for identifying bioactive compounds responsible for the observed protective effects and supports further phytochemical investigations. Additionally, the study creates a foundation for future in-vivo experiments and clinical research aimed at validating the safety and efficacy of Pergularia daemia in real biological systems. From a broader perspective, this research supports the growing interest in natural and plant-based therapeutics as alternatives to conventional drugs, which often have limitations such as side effects and high costs. The outcomes of this project may aid in the development of novel herbal formulations or lead compounds for liver disease management, particularly in conditions like liver cirrhosis where effective treatment options remain limited. )
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
