1016 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 634 0 R
/E (Research on hepatoprotective agents has expanded significantly in recent years, particularly focusing on plant-derived compounds due to their safety profile and therapeutic potential. Several studies have demonstrated that medicinal plants rich in antioxidants can effectively protect liver cells from damage caused by toxins, drugs, and oxidative stress. These studies provide a strong scientific basis for exploring herbal remedies in the management of liver diseases such as cirrhosis. Sharma et al. \(2022\) investigated the hepatoprotective activity of plant extracts using in-vitro models and reported that flavonoid-rich compounds significantly improved hepatocyte viability by reducing oxidative stress. Similarly, Kumar et al. \(2021\) evaluated the effect of diosgenin in an experimental liver injury model and found that it effectively reduced liver enzyme levels and prevented cellular damage through its antioxidant and anti-inflammatory mechanisms. These findings highlight the importance of natural compounds in protecting liver function. Recent studies have also focused on the role of oxidative stress in liver damage. Singh et al. \(2023\) demonstrated that excessive production of reactive oxygen species leads to lipid peroxidation and cellular injury, which can be mitigated by antioxidant-rich plant extracts. In another study, Rao et al. \(2020\) emphasized the significance of herbal medicines in reducing hepatotoxicity induced by chemicals such as carbon tetrachloride and paracetamol. Specific research on Pergularia daemia has revealed its potential pharmacological activities. Devi et al. \(2021\) reported that the plant possesses strong antioxidant and anti-inflammatory properties due to the presence of bioactive compounds like saponins and flavonoids. Furthermore, Prakash et al. \(2022\) highlighted its traditional use in treating liver disorders and suggested its possible hepatoprotective role, although detailed in-vitro validation remains limited. Overall, the reviewed literature indicates that plant-based compounds, particularly those with antioxidant properties, play )
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
