<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 635 0 R
/E (The use of medicinal plants for the treatment of liver disorders has been an integral part of traditional healthcare systems for centuries. In recent years, scientific research has increasingly validated the hepatoprotective properties of various plant-derived compounds. These plants are rich in phytochemicals such as flavonoids, alkaloids, tannins, and saponins, which are known to exhibit antioxidant, anti-inflammatory, and cytoprotective activities. Such properties play )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1024 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 635 0 R
/E (a crucial role in protecting liver cells from damage caused by toxins, drugs, and environmental pollutants. Several experimental studies have demonstrated that plant extracts can significantly improve liver function by reducing enzyme leakage, enhancing antioxidant defense mechanisms, and stabilizing cellular membranes \(Seki et al., 2021; Tilg et al., 2022; Arab et al., 2022; Czaja, 2022\). In in-vitro models, hepatocyte cell lines exposed to toxic agents have shown improved viability when treated with herbal extracts, indicating their protective effects at the cellular level. Moreover, many plant-based compounds have been found to inhibit lipid peroxidation, a key process involved in liver damage, thereby preserving the structural integrity of hepatocytes. The growing interest in plant-based hepatoprotective agents is also driven by the limitations of conventional drugs, which often have side effects and limited long-term efficacy. Herbal medicines, on the other hand, are generally considered safer and more accessible, especially in developing regions \(Bansal et al., 2022; Alshawsh et al., 2021\). As a result, there is a continuous effort to identify and evaluate new medicinal plants with potential liver-protective properties. This body of research provides a strong foundation for further studies, including the investigation of lesser-explored plants like Pergularia daemia, to establish their therapeutic relevance in liver disease management. )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1025 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 636 0 R
/Pg 31 0 R
/A [2651 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1026 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 636 0 R
/E (10 )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1027 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 636 0 R
/E (dysfunction and death \(Singh et al., 2023\). In the liver, oxidative stress is often triggered by exposure to toxins, alcohol, drugs, and viral infections. One of the key processes associated with oxidative stress in liver injury is lipid peroxidation, which involves the oxidative degradation of cell membrane lipids. This process compromises membrane integrity, leading to increased permeability and leakage of intracellular enzymes such as alanine transaminase \(ALT\) and aspartate transaminase \(AST\) into the bloodstream \(Lee et al., 2021\). Elevated levels of these enzymes are commonly used as biomarkers of liver damage. In addition, oxidative stress can activate inflammatory pathways and promote fibrosis, further contributing to the progression of liver cirrhosis. Numerous studies have highlighted the importance of antioxidants in mitigating oxidative stress and protecting liver cells. Natural compounds with antioxidant properties can scavenge free radicals, enhance endogenous antioxidant enzymes, and reduce cellular damage. In in-vitro studies, antioxidant-rich plant extracts have been shown to significantly reduce ROS levels and improve cell survival under toxic conditions \(Lee et al., 2021\). These findings underscore the potential of targeting oxidative stress as a therapeutic agent in liver disease management and support the exploration of plants like Pergularia daemia, which are believed to possess strong antioxidant activity. )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1028 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 636 0 R
/E (Diosgenin is a naturally occurring steroidal saponin that has attracted significant attention in the field of pharmacology due to its wide range of therapeutic properties. It is commonly found in plants such as yams and certain medicinal herbs and is known for its antioxidant, anti- inflammatory, anticancer, and hepatoprotective activities. Among these, its role in liver protection has been extensively studied, making it a valuable compound in experimental hepatology. The hepatoprotective effect of diosgenin is primarily attributed to its ability to reduce oxidative stress and modulate inflammatory responses. It acts as a potent free radical scavenger, thereby preventing the formation of reactive oxygen species and reducing lipid peroxidation in liver cells. Additionally, diosgenin has been shown to enhance the activity of endogenous antioxidant enzymes such as superoxide dismutase and catalase, which further strengthens the cellular defense system. These actions help in maintaining the structural and functional integrity of hepatocytes under toxic conditions. Another important )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1029 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
