/E (aspect of diosgenin is its ability to regulate signalling pathways involved in cell survival, apoptosis, and inflammation. By modulating these pathways, it promotes cell regeneration and reduces the extent of liver injury. Due to its well-documented efficacy and reproducibility in experimental studies, diosgenin is widely used as a standard reference compound for evaluating the hepatoprotective activity of new drugs and plant extracts. Its inclusion in comparative studies provides a reliable benchmark, allowing researchers to assess the relative effectiveness of test substances such as Pergularia daemia extract in protecting liver cells from damage. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1033 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 637 0 R
/E (Pergularia daemia is a medicinal plant that has been traditionally used in various indigenous systems of medicine for the treatment of multiple health conditions, including liver disorders. Despite its long history of use, scientific research on this plant is still emerging, with recent studies beginning to explore its pharmacological properties in greater detail. Preliminary investigations have revealed that Pergularia daemia contains a variety of bioactive compounds, including flavonoids, alkaloids, glycosides, and saponins, which contribute to its therapeutic potential. Research studies have reported that the plant exhibits significant antioxidant activity, which is essential for protecting cells against oxidative damage. In experimental models, extracts of Pergularia daemia have been shown to reduce the levels of reactive oxygen species and inhibit lipid peroxidation, thereby preserving cell membrane integrity. These findings suggest that the plant may play a protective role in conditions associated with oxidative stress, such as liver diseases. In addition to its antioxidant properties, Pergularia daemia has also demonstrated anti-inflammatory and antimicrobial activities, further supporting its medicinal value. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
