/E (Another major gap in the existing literature is the lack of comparative studies between Pergularia daemia and well-established hepatoprotective compounds. While several studies have reported the beneficial effects of various medicinal plants, they often do not include a standard reference for comparison. Without such a benchmark, it becomes difficult to assess the relative efficacy and therapeutic potential of the plant extract. Diosgenin, a widely recognized hepatoprotective compound, is commonly used as a standard due to its proven antioxidant and cytoprotective properties. However, very few studies have compared the activity of Pergularia daemia directly with Diosgenin in the same experimental setup. This gap reduces the scientific reliability and applicability of the findings. A well-designed comparative analysis is necessary to determine whether the plant extract can match or approach the effectiveness of established compounds, thereby strengthening its potential for therapeutic use. )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1051 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 639 0 R
/E (interaction between the plant extract and liver cells, including mechanisms related to cell survival, oxidative stress, and enzyme regulation. The absence of such focused investigations limits the ability to establish a clear scientific basis for its hepatoprotective claims. Therefore, there is a significant need to conduct systematic in-vitro studies to evaluate the protective effects of Pergularia daemia under controlled experimental conditions. )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1052 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 639 0 R
/E (While some studies have reported the beneficial effects of medicinal plants in liver protection, there is often a lack of detailed investigation into the underlying mechanisms of action. Understanding how a plant extract exerts its protective effects at the molecular and cellular levels is crucial for its development as a therapeutic agent. In the case of Pergularia daemia, limited information is available regarding its influence on oxidative stress pathways, enzyme regulation, and cellular signalling processes involved in hepatocyte protection. Most studies provide general observations without exploring specific biochemical or molecular interactions. This lack of mechanistic clarity restricts the ability to fully understand its pharmacological potential and limits its acceptance in mainstream medical research. )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
