/E (Fresh leaves of Pergularia daemia were collected from a clean and unpolluted natural habitat to ensure the quality and purity of the plant material. The collection was carried out during the appropriate season to obtain fully matured leaves with maximum phytochemical content. Care was taken to avoid contamination with soil, dust, or other plant species. Immediately after collection, the leaves were washed thoroughly with distilled water to remove any adhering impurities and then shade-dried at room temperature to preserve their bioactive constituents \(Devi et al., 2021\). Direct sunlight was avoided during drying to prevent degradation of heat- sensitive compounds. )
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1066 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 641 0 R
/E (Figure 4: Fresh collection of Pergularia daemia Leaves )
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1067 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 641 0 R
/E (The dried plant material was then coarsely powdered using a mechanical grinder and stored in airtight containers to protect it from moisture and environmental exposure. The powdered sample was labelled and preserved under suitable conditions until further use. For authentication, the plant specimen was verified by a qualified botanist or submitted to a recognized herbarium, where its taxonomical identity was confirmed based on morphological characteristics. Proper documentation of the plant, including its scientific name, family, and collection details, was maintained. This step ensured the reliability and reproducibility of the study by confirming the correct identification of Pergularia daemia used for experimental analysis. )
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
