endobj
1119 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 647 0 R
/E (To assess the hepatoprotective activity of Pergularia daemia, a series of biochemical and cellular assays were performed to evaluate cell viability, enzyme leakage, oxidative stress, and lipid peroxidation. These parameters are widely recognized indicators of liver cell health and damage. The MTT assay was used to determine cell viability. This colorimetric assay is based on the ability of viable cells to reduce MTT reagent into a purple-coloured formazan product. The intensity of the colour, measured using a spectrophotometer, is directly proportional to the number of living cells \(Das et al., 2022\). This assay provided a quantitative measure of the protective effect of the plant extract against induced toxicity. Liver-specific enzyme assays, including alanine transaminase \(ALT\) and aspartate transaminase \(AST\), were conducted to evaluate cellular damage \(Lee et al., 2021\). Elevated levels of these enzymes in the culture medium indicate membrane damage and leakage from hepatocytes. A reduction in enzyme levels in treated groups suggests protective activity. Oxidative stress was assessed by measuring reactive oxygen species \(ROS\) levels within the cells. Increased ROS production is a key indicator of cellular stress and damage \(Wang et al., 2022\). The ability of the plant extract to reduce ROS levels reflects its antioxidant potential. Lipid peroxidation was evaluated by measuring malondialdehyde \(MDA\) levels, a byproduct of membrane lipid degradation \(Wu et al., 2021\). )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1120 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 647 0 R
/E (Following the field collection shown earlier \(figure.5\), the leaves are shade-dried and ground. This image shows the final physical representation. Crucially, the image captures the dark, rich green, slightly viscous appearance of the concentrated plant extract, which is ready to be weighed on the simple balance shown in figure.9 The control group consisted of untreated HepG2 cells maintained under normal culture conditions, serving as a baseline for comparison. The toxic control group included cells exposed only to the hepatotoxic agent \(CCl4 or paracetamol\), representing maximum cellular damage without any protective treatment. The standard group was treated with diosgenin along with the toxic agent, providing a reference for hepatoprotective activity. The test groups were treated with varying concentrations of Pergularia daemia extract in the presence of the toxic agent. )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [3 5 4]
>>
endobj
1121 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 647 0 R
/E (This allowed for the evaluation of dose-dependent effects and identification of the most effective concentration. Each experimental condition was performed in triplicate to ensure reproducibility and statistical validity. This grouping strategy enabled a comprehensive comparison between untreated, damaged, standard-treated, and plant-treated cells, thereby facilitating a clear assessment of the hepatoprotective potential of the extract. )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
