1267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 651 0 R
/E (The measurement of liver enzymes ALT and AST serves as a reliable indicator of hepatocellular damage. As shown in Table 2, the toxic control group exhibited a substantial increase in enzyme levels \(ALT: 68 U/L and AST: 72 U/L\), indicating significant membrane damage and leakage of intracellular enzymes into the extracellular medium. )
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1268 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 651 0 R
/E (Treatment with Pergularia daemia extract resulted in a progressive reduction in enzyme levels. The low-dose group showed moderate improvement, while the medium and high-dose groups demonstrated a more pronounced decrease. At the highest concentration, ALT and AST levels were reduced to 32 U/L and 36 U/L, respectively, which are comparable to the values observed in the diosgenin-treated group \(ALT: 30 U/L, AST: 34 U/L\). This trend is clearly depicted in Figure 13, where enzyme levels decline steadily from the toxic control to the treated groups. The reduction in enzyme leakage indicates stabilization of the hepatocyte membrane and preservation of cellular integrity \(Lee et al., 2021\). )
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1269 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 651 0 R
/E (The ability of the extract to reduce ALT and AST levels suggests that it may protect against structural damage and maintain normal cellular function \(Gupta et al., 2021\). This effect is likely mediated by its antioxidant and anti-inflammatory properties, which help in preventing cellular injury and promoting repair mechanisms. )
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
