/E (Oxidative stress is a key factor in liver injury, and its assessment was carried out by measuring intracellular ROS levels. As indicated in Table 3, the toxic control group exhibited )
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1370 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 652 0 R
/E (a significant increase in ROS production \(185%\), reflecting severe oxidative stress and cellular damage. Treatment with Pergularia daemia extract led to a dose-dependent reduction in ROS levels. The low concentration reduced ROS to 150%, while medium and high concentrations further decreased it to 130% and 115%, respectively. The standard diosgenin group showed ROS levels of 110%, which is very close to the highest extract concentration. The trend is visually represented in Figure 14, where a sharp peak is observed in the toxic control group, followed by )
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1371 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 652 0 R
/E (a gradual decline in treated groups. )
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1372 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 652 0 R
/E (This clearly demonstrates the antioxidant potential of the extract. The reduction in ROS levels suggests that the phytochemicals present in Pergularia daemia are capable of scavenging free radicals and enhancing the cellular antioxidant defense system \(Wang et al., 2022., Singh et al., 2023\). This activity plays a crucial role in preventing oxidative damage to cellular components and maintaining overall cell health. )
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
