1446 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 653 0 R
/E (Lipid peroxidation is a major consequence of oxidative stress and is commonly assessed by measuring MDA levels. As shown in Table 4, the toxic control group exhibited a significant increase in MDA levels \(5.9 nmol/mg protein\), indicating extensive damage to cell membranes. Treatment with Pergularia daemia extract resulted in a gradual decrease in MDA levels across all concentrations. The high-dose group showed a value of 2.5 nmol/mg protein, which is close to the diosgenin group \(2.2 nmol/mg protein\). Figure 15 illustrates this decreasing trend, where MDA levels are significantly reduced in treated groups compared to the toxic control. This indicates that the extract effectively inhibits lipid peroxidation and protects membrane integrity \(Mehta et al., 2022\). The ability to reduce MDA levels highlights the role of the extract in preventing oxidative degradation of lipids. This protective effect is essential for maintaining cell structure and function, particularly in hepatocytes, which are highly susceptible to oxidative damage \(Gupta et al., 2021\). )
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
